"OverallSummary": "This case involves Kim R. Kunz, a registered representative with WestPark Capital, Inc., who recommended that two retail customers invest in GWG L Bonds, which were speculative, unrated debt securities. GWG L Bonds were high-risk investments issued by GWG Holdings, Inc., a company with a history of net losses that eventually defaulted on its obligations and filed for bankruptcy in 2022. For Customer A, Kunz's November 2019 recommendation resulted in over 65% of the customer's liquid net worth being concentrated in alternative investments, violating FINRA's suitability rule (Rule 2111) as this recommendation occurred before Reg BI took effect. For Customer B, Kunz's February 2021 recommendation of an additional $147,000 investment in L Bonds (after an initial $50,000 investment) resulted in over 30% of the customer's liquid net worth being concentrated in these securities. This recommendation violated Regulation Best Interest's Care Obligation because it was not in the customer's best interest given her moderate risk tolerance and investment objectives. The Care Obligation violation was determined through an analysis of: (1) the customer's investment profile (moderate risk tolerance, income objective, no speculation objective); (2) the high-risk nature of the recommended investment; (3) the resulting concentration level exceeding 30% of liquid net worth; and (4) the timing of the recommendation after Reg BI took effect on June 30, 2020. As a result, Kunz was ordered to pay a $7,500 fine, $1,927 in restitution representing the commission from Customer A's investment, and received a three-month suspension."
